    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6642A\n\nSPONSOR: KAPLAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law, in relation to a report by the divi-\nsion for small business; and to amend chapter 731 of the laws of 2019\namending the executive law relating to regulatory fines for small busi-\nnesses, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation extends until December 20, 2024 the law allowing small\nbusinesses to cure a first violation of certain agency regulations, this\nlegislation also creates a report aimed at detailing the effectiveness\nof this law.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new subdivision 4 to Section 170-c of the executive law\nto require every state agency to provide to the division of small busi-\nness information detailing (a) how many cure periods or were offered and\nhow many small businesses made use of this option (b) how many cure\nperiods were successfully completed and resulted in avoidance of a\npenalty (c) what guidance, information, and assistance with compliance\nwas provided (d) average length of time granted to correct first time\nviolations. This section also requires a report to be created compiling\nthis information that will be posted on their website as well as the\nTemporary President of the Senate and the Speaker of the Assembly.\n\nSection 2 is necessary language because of the underlying chapter\naddressed in Section 1 of this new bill.\n\nSection 2 extends this law until December 20, 2024 Section 3 is the\neffective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2019 the Legislature Passed and the Governor signed into law, impact-\nful legislation that allows a small business the opportunity to cure a\npotential violation without penalty. This incredibly important small\nbusiness legislation is set to expire and should be extended.\n\nFurther, this legislation also would require a report to be created\ndetailing how impactful this law has been so that the public, and the\nLegislature can better understand the laws true impact upon the small\nbusiness community.\n\nNew York's small businesses are heavily regulated and are not always\naware of the numerous current and new regulations. Various regulations\ncan cost small businesses thousands of dollars in implementation and\npenalties. By allowing our small businesses the opportunity to cure as\nopposed to an immediate fine, the state would be improving communication\nwith our small businesses and working to improve our business climate.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately and shall be deemed expired December 20, 2024.\n",
